nodes.body:
@NolenLiang Thanks—your NIXL follow-up and hardware measurements would be welcome. This PR is focused on core sharing with the built-in CPU offloading path; NIXL support isn't currently being implemented here.

Building a separate follow-up against this branch makes sense, with the caveat that the contract may change during maintainer review. NIXL will need explicit compatibility and lease handling, including keeping transfers alive after owner cancellation and releasing every producer-side request.

Please share the cold 1P+1D GRPO baseline: duplicate transfers, transferred bytes, unique destination GPU blocks, and TTFT, together with concurrency, prefix length, model, and TP configuration. That would help establish the benefit and guide validation.

@orozery Thanks for the suggestion. Updated in `367746fff7`: the block pool now owns the pending-prefix index, longest exact-prefix lookup, and transfer-owned block pins. Pending loads remain separate from ordinary readable cache hits; failed entries are excluded from discovery, and completion removes the entry and releases the pins.

The scheduler retains connector opt-in/eligibility, request waiters, and completion/failure-policy handling. The existing request cancellation behavior is preserved. During a two-reader load, each block now has three references: two request references plus one transfer pin; completion releases the transfer reference without allocating additional GPU blocks.

The focused suite passes 636 tests, including new pool tests for retention after all request references are released, prevention of reuse/reset while loading, group isolation, duplicate registration, failed lookup suppression, and pin cleanup. All applicable changed-file hooks, including mypy, pass. The PR description reflects the revised boundary and outstanding GPU lifecycle/model-output validation; the older external GPU evidence does not validate this revision.

@njhill Could you review the core/block-pool boundary, or suggest the appropriate core reviewer?

